<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Puyi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">abdicated <a href="/years/1912">1912</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-aisin-gioro">House of Aisin Gioro</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Puyi was the last emperor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a>, enthroned as a small child. Following the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinhai-revolution">Xinhai Revolution</a>, he abdicated on 12 February 1912, ending more than two millennia of imperial rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. He later served as the nominal ruler of the Japanese puppet state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchukuo">Manchukuo</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/puyi.jpg" alt="Puyi" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Puyi,_Manchukuo_period_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Puyi was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> on 7 February 1906, the son of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> prince Zaifeng, Prince Chun, and of Youlan, daughter of Ronglu, an ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empress-dowager-cixi">Empress Dowager Cixi</a>. His uncle, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangxu-emperor">Guangxu Emperor</a>, was his predecessor. In 1908 Cixi chose him to succeed the childless Guangxu, and he ascended the throne at the age of two with his father as regent. After the garrison at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuhan">Wuhan</a> mutinied on 10 October 1911, the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shikai">Yuan Shikai</a> was sent to crush the revolt but could not, since opinion had turned against a dynasty that many believed had lost the mandate of heaven.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Empress Dowager Longyu, regent from 6 December 1911, endorsed the edict of abdication on 12 February 1912 under a deal brokered by Yuan. Under the Articles of Favourable Treatment Puyi kept his imperial title and remained in the northern half of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/forbidden-city">Forbidden City</a>. The general Zhang Xun restored him to the throne from 1 to 12 July 1917, and from 1919 the Scottish scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reginald-johnston">Reginald Johnston</a> tutored him. On 5 November 1924 the warlord Feng Yuxiang abolished his title and expelled him from the palace, and in 1925 he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tianjin">Tianjin</a>.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shenyang">Mukden</a> incident of 18 September 1931 the Japanese Kwantung Army proposed that he head a Manchurian state. He was installed as Chief Executive of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchukuo">Manchukuo</a> on 1 March 1932 and crowned Emperor of Manchukuo, with the regnal title Kangde, on 1 March 1934. On 9 August 1945 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> entered Manchukuo and its armies overwhelmed the Kwantung Army.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The Soviets took Puyi to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>, and in 1946 he testified at the Tokyo War Crimes Tribunal. He was repatriated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 1950 and, after his release in 1959, published an autobiography ghostwritten by Li Wenda and worked as a gardener and then as a researcher. Red Guards burned copies of his book during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultural-revolution">Cultural Revolution</a>, but Premier Zhou Enlai protected him. Puyi died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> on 17 October 1967, aged 61, of kidney cancer and heart disease.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Puyi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>To create an air of legitimacy the last emperor of China, <b>Puyi</b>, was brought in with his followers, among them the Qing loyalist Zheng Xiaoxu.</blockquote><a href="/empire/manchukuo">Manchukuo · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>It led to the abdication of the six-year-old <b>Puyi</b> on 12 February 1912, which ended more than two thousand years of imperial rule in China.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qing-empire">Qing Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>On 12 February 1912 the six-year-old <b>Xuantong Emperor</b>, Puyi, abdicated, ending more than two thousand years of imperial rule.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-china">Republic of China · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Puyi</b>'s abdication in 1912 ended its role as China's political centre, though he stayed in the Inner Court until a coup evicted him in 1924, and the Palace Museum was founded there in 1925.</blockquote><a href="/work/forbidden-city">Forbidden City · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>The Empress Dowager Longyu issued the edict of abdication for the six-year-old <b>Puyi</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-12">12 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Its commissioners included the scholar-administrators James Stewart Lockhart and Reginald Johnston, former tutor to the last Qing emperor, <b>Puyi</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/weihaiwei">Weihaiwei</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Xicheng District, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in Dongcheng District, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.91,116.36&amp;year=1912" aria-label="Xicheng District on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7416 736 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Puyi"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7866 1006h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7866 1006h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=39.91,116.36&amp;year=1912">What was here in 1912? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
